dits left”\nWhen the balance reaches 25 credits or fewer, on Standard as well as Expert, the composer says so once and the ring takes the warning colour, so the boundary is expected. Nothing else changes and sending works as usual.\n\n## 3b · The next Expert message can’t be paid\nCaught before sending. A chat keeps its model, so there is no Send with Standard: Send is disabled, one line says why, and there is one way forward. Get more credits opens the pricing page (5a), which says how packs and plans differ, so nobody has to choose between them from one line here. The typed text is untouched.\n\n## 3c · The rare gateway 402: the failed-message card\nOnly a race between two sends reaches this. The card is the app’s .message-failure with the 402’s words and, as on 3b, only Get more credits. The message goes back into the composer, so nothing typed is lost.\n\n## 3e · Picture in a Standard chat\nThe Standard model can’t see images, so the staged row’s second line is a warning that the photo won’t go with the message. Send stays enabled, because the words can still go on their own. Remove is the only action on the row. The picker option stays as short as on 2b.\n\n## 4a · Preferences › Account, opened over Prepare\nThe window at its usual 640, over the screen it opened from, at the top of the section: the plan and Get more credits, the total, and a bar for each kind of credit. 4b shows the whole section.\n\n## 4b · Account, the whole section: first month plus a pack\nA bar for each kind of credit, as on Claude’s and ChatGPT’s usage screens, with what is left and when it expires. Bought credits get a bar like the others. Below, as on Lovable’s Plans & credit usage, usage per day with totals per chat, not a row per message. Hovering a bar shows that day. Purchases and rewards (4c) sit in the same list. The alert’s control is the Preferences checkbox the app already uses. Turns the app starts are free and don’t appear.\n\n## 4c · Account on Workshop, an Obico Premium Subscriber\nOne bar, for the plan’s monthly credits; bought credits would add a second. Standard messages cost 0 credits, so they appear in Usage at 0 and there is no ceiling to count. The discounted price is shown as the price, detected, never entered.\n\n## 5a · The pricing page, in the browser\nThree cards named as decided, each stating its expiry rule in its own words. The one sentence at the top says what a message costs. Dollars belong here; Get more credits on the composer and in Account opens this page. The only other places that name dollars are the savings alert’s sums and the plan line in Account. The launch note is on the pack card with its end date (31 Jan is a placeholder for JUSPRIN_LAUNCH_DOUBLE_UNTIL). Checkout is Chargebee’s hosted page.\n\n## 5b · The same page for an Obico Premium Subscriber\nThe discounted price is the price, $8 and $16, with one line saying why and no coupon to type. Pay as you go keeps its price: the discount is on subscriptions.\n\n## 6a · First launch after the first month: what they have, not what they lost\nA new chat starts on Standard, and free credits get the same ring as any other credits; hovering it shows Free credits, 50 of 50 left. No wall, no modal, no notice about what expired, no “upgrade”. A chat already on Expert stays on Expert, because a chat keeps its model, so it carries on from these credits and shows 3b once fewer than 5 are left.\n\n## 6b · In a new chat, Expert is one click away and shows its price\nThe free credits pay for either tier, so Expert can be used from them. Picking Expert with too few credits is allowed: the composer then says “You have 0 credits; Expert needs 5 a message.” with Get more credits, and Send stays disabled. The menu stays open to Standard until the person sends their first message.\n\n## 7a · Savings alert: Pay as you go to Hobby\nOnce, above the composer, dismissable, with the saving in dollars. On by default, with its switch in Preferences › Account. The sums are one tap away.\n\n## 7b · The arithmetic, one tap away\nThe line opens in place to the one sentence of arithmetic. Nothing else moves and the person can keep typing.\n\n## 8a · The print report button on the Print history entry\nA report is about one G-code, so the button sits on that G-code’s entry, beside Print again and Export. It is not in the chat: by the time a print is done the person is usually in another chat, or back hours later, and can’t be expected to find the original one (Kenneth, 2026-10-27). No button while the print is running. An entry that was only exported has it too, because a print from a USB stick never reports back. One report per G-code.\n\n## 8b · The report form, over Print history\nOpens from the entry and names it, so the person knows which version they are reporting. Says what earns the reward: a photo of the printed object and a few sentences. The outcome is the person’s own answer for the report, not a print state: Print history stays without a failure state (decided 2026-10-27).\n\n## 8c · After the report is sent\nThe button becomes a row on the entry, and that row is where the person sees the credits: this screen has no composer, so a “+25 credits” line there would go unseen. The row states that a report exists and nothing about how the print went.\n\n## 8d · The way to the button from Home\nPeople come back to Home with the part in hand, so the offer has to be visible before they open Print history. A project card gets the line when its latest G-code has finished and has no report; two weeks later the line goes away (my guess at the limit). It opens Print history filtered to that project. Home’s other ways in stay as the Print history page draws them. In a chat, the one-line G-code marker keeps its “history” link and gets no button.\n\n## 9 · Model, thinking and the credits ring, state by state, light and dark\nThe composer’s new parts on their own, after the screens they live in. Toolbars are at the pane’s default width.\n\n## 0 · Five rules wherever a person meets credits\nThe boards themselves sit with their flows: On boarding experience, Compositions (beside the Composer), Agent setup & Preferences and Print history. Search “credits” to find them all.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sandboxed":false,"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b9d00b00-a17c-457c-bb3c-35bff40a4334","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/kenneth/Projects/JusPrin/src/slic3r/GUI/JusPrin/AgentUI/src/components","sessionId":"473348c5-5f94-4e4d-9ad9-c7e9dd10b438","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"jusprin-newui","slug":"glimmering-doodling-lovelace"}
